In article <Byu0sG.9nD@unix.amherst.edu> twpierce@unix.amherst.edu (Tim Pierce) writes:
>In article <BytKE2.MGw@mtholyoke.edu> jbotz@mtholyoke.edu (Jurgen Botz) writes:
>
>>In article <1992Dec4.211934.8997@pica.army.mil> drears@claudius.pica.army.mil (Dennis G Rears ) writes:
>>>I am the moderator for comp.society.privacy and recently found
>>>a posting that I did not approve.
>>
>>This is the *only* valid reason for canceling
>>someone elses post.
>
>Ooh ... gonna get yourself in trouble there, I bet. :-)
>
>Suppose I find that someone else has forged an article in my name?

8-)  Ok, I'll back off from that claim a bit.  I think forgeries are
better handled with a follow-up exposing the forgery as such, but I
certainly wouldn't flame someone for canceling a post that was forged
in their name.  However, canceling a forgery in *your* name isn't really
canceling someone else's article!  It's canceling an article that was
purportedly from you anyway, so it's not really opposed to what I was
getting at when I stated that the only time that it's appropriate for
a third party to cancel a post is in the case of a moderator canceling
unapproved articles to his/her group.

Another reader sent me mail suggesting that it is appropriate to
cancel "illegal" posts, but there I would beg to differ...  what's
illegal, and where?  This horse has been beaten to death just recently
over the "pedophilia" posts in a.b.p.e, and I think that regardless of
one's individial position in this issue, the issue needs to be addressed
in a more general way and not on a case-by-case basis by self-appointed
net.police canceling posts.

Mainly the question of canceling other people's articles boils down to
a question of ownership and authority.  By the time something has
propagated it is outside of the sphere of authority of anyone but the
individual admins of the systems the article is stored on.  The rest
is net.convention and the cooperative spirit of the net: Since the
article is owned by the author, the author has somewhat of an implicit
license to cancel the article, and moderators have an implicit license
over what happens in the group which they moderate.  These are things
that I agreed to by joining my system to the net... however in almost
all other cases I would consider someone canceling articles as a
violation of *my* domain of authority: my newsspool.  If you cancel an
article that you consider illegal, for example, it makes me want to
say: "Hey, what are you doing?  Do you have a warrant?  Who gave you
the authority to mess with data stored on my disks?"  I mean, how would
you feel if you had some pirated software on your PC, and your neighbor
were to just walk into your home and start erasing it?  I believe that in
the the USA at least that's even more illegal than software piracy.

